WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.260
Parameter count gives a rough indication of model scale,

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:08.340
but loaded memory also depends on numerical precision, quantization format, context cache,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.760
and runtime overhead.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.460
Quantization stores weights with fewer bits,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.520
reducing memory demand and often improving practical local speed.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.840
The tradeoff is potential quality loss, which varies by model, task,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.235
and quantization method.

00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:22.060
A larger model is not automatically better for a local workflow.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:25.920
If it barely loads, there may be no headroom for context, concurrent requests,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.584
or graphical applications.

00:00:27.584 --> 00:00:30.680
Partial CPU offload can make it run but reduce generation speed.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.960
A smaller model that remains fully accelerated may respond sooner,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.533
support a larger useful context, and stay stable through a long session.

00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:40.140
Compare candidate models on representative tasks.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.820
Use the same prompts and evaluate accuracy, instruction following, latency,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.615
generation speed, and memory.

00:00:45.615 --> 00:00:48.200
Test more than one quantization when quality matters.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.470
Moderate quantization often provides a useful balance,

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.390
while aggressive compression may be appropriate for less sensitive tasks or limited

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.035
hardware.

00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:58.900
File size alone does not prove runtime compatibility or final memory use.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.670
Choose a model that runs well, not one that merely starts.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.620
Leave memory headroom, select a context appropriate to the job,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.760
and consider specialized smaller models for coding, embeddings, or narrow domains.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.680
Optimization is the combination of acceptable output quality and dependable resource use.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.520
The right model is the smallest configuration that meets the requirement consistently,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.670
not the largest one the loading screen can survive.
